.com\/facebookresearch\/","inherit","open-in-new-tab",["AI Research","GitHub"],null,"click_menu","nav_research-GitHub","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_f_Qs",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Blog","\/blog\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["Resources","Blog"],null,"click_menu","nav_blog","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_g_WW",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Learning Hub","\/learn\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["Resources","Learning Hub"],null,"click_menu","nav_learning_hub","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_h_vS",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Demos","https:\/\/aidemos.meta.com\/","inherit","open-in-new-tab",["Resources","Demos"],null,"click_menu","nav_demos","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_i_2Z",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Overview","\/about\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["About","Overview"],null,"click_menu","nav_about_overview","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_j_le",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Open Source","\/opensourceai\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["About","Open Source"],null,"click_menu","nav_about_open_source","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_k_zb",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Careers","https:\/\/www.metacareers.com\/","inherit","open-in-new-tab",["About","Careers"],null,"click_menu","nav_about_careers","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_l_8L",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Muse","\/muse\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["Products","Muse"],null,"click_menu","nav_muse","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_m_Ri",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Meta AI","\/meta-ai\/assistant\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["Products","Meta AI"],null,"click_menu","nav_meta_ai_overview","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_n_0M",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Media Generation","\/meta-ai\/media-generation\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["Products","Media Generation"],null,"click_menu","nav_meta_ai_media_generation","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_o_xs",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Vibes","\/meta-ai\/vibes\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["Products","Vibes"],null,"click_menu","nav_vibes","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_p_1Q",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Overview","\/research\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["AI Research","Overview"],null,"click_menu","nav_research-overview","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_q_Eg",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Projects","\/research\/#projects","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["AI Research","Projects"],null,"click_menu","nav_research_projects","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_r_jh",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Resources & Tools","\/resources\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["AI Research","Resources & Tools"],null,"click_menu","nav_research-Resources-and-Tools","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_s_Uo",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Publications","\/results\/?content_types[0]=publication","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["AI Research","Publications"],null,"click_menu","nav_research-Publications","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_t_2v",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["GitHub","https:\/\/github.com\/facebookresearch\/","inherit","open-in-new-tab",["AI Research","GitHub"],null,"click_menu","nav_research-GitHub","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_u_sK",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Blog","\/blog\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["Resources","Blog"],null,"click_menu","nav_blog","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_v_cs",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Learning Hub","\/learn\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["Resources","Learning Hub"],null,"click_menu","nav_learning_hub","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_w_c4",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Demos","https:\/\/aidemos.meta.com\/","inherit","open-in-new-tab",["Resources","Demos"],null,"click_menu","nav_demos","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_x_m6",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Overview","\/about\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["About","Overview"],null,"click_menu","nav_about_overview","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_y_Pq",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Open Source","\/opensourceai\/","inherit","open-in-current-tab",["About","Open Source"],null,"click_menu","nav_about_open_source","default","all","underline"],1],["__inst_24928c59_0_z_YW",["AIDropdownNavLinkConfig"],["Careers","https:\/\/www.metacareers.com\/","inherit","open-in-new-tab",["About","Careers"],null,"click_menu","nav_about_careers","default","all","underline"],1]],"markup":[["__markup_97adea0c_0_0_5D",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">Del Pia and Khajavirad introduced the complete edge relaxation for binary polynomial optimization and generalized triangle inequalities for length-three \u03b1-cycles. We study the relaxation obtained by adding all switchings of these inequalities to the complete edge relaxation on the completed support of a single length-three \u03b1-cycle. If H = cl(&#123;e1, e2, e3&#125;) and A = (e1 \u2229 e2) \\ e3, B = (e2 \u2229 e3) \\ e1, C = (e1 \u2229 e3) \\ e2, then the cycle-based relaxation is equal to the multilinear polytope if and only if |A| = |B| = |C| = 1. The same characterization remains true for the stronger relaxation obtained by imposing the switched generalized triangle inequalities for every no-private length-three \u03b1-cycle contained in H. No restriction is needed on the common intersection or on the private vertices of the three maximal edges. The positive direction is proved by a slice-wise gluing argument, while the negative direction lifts a four-variable parity obstruction and shows that it survives every applicable switched triangle inequality. We remark on the use of AI in this research, see Statement of AI Use.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_97adea0c_0_1_vb",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">We study finite-dimensional solvable evolution algebras, that is, evolution algebras for which their derived series terminates at zero. We disprove a conjecture by Garc\u00eda-Mart\u00ednez and P\u00e9rez-Rodr\u00edguez. Then, in terms of idempotent subspaces, we propose a novel characterization of solvable evolution algebras as well as of evolution algebras without nonzero idempotent elements. We remark on the use of AI in this research, see Statement of AI Use.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_97adea0c_0_2_B6",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">We generalize the recently demonstrated equivalence between local heights and boundary propagators on the Tate curve to any curve over a p-adic local field with semistable reduction. Specifically, we show that the boundary two-point function of a p-adic string theory with the curve as the asymptotic boundary coincides with the N\u00e9ron local height pairing on degree zero divisors. We remark on the use of AI in this research, see Statement of AI use.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"],["__markup_97adea0c_0_3_RM",{"__html":"\u003Cp class=\"_8w6f _8xm4 _8w61 _8w6h\">We disprove a conjecture of M. Kida by exhibiting a finite semiabelian group of order 384 that is not monomial. The example is given by GAP\u2019s SmallGroups library as\nSmallGroup(384, 20127) which is isomorphic to the group ((C2 \u00d7 ((C2 \u00d7 Q8) \u22ca C2)) \u22ca C2) \u22ca C3.\nWe remark on the use of AI in this research, see Statement of AI Use.\u003C\/p>"},1,"HTML"]],"elements":[["__elem_c37bd704_0_0_9X","u_0_0_FI",1],["__elem_c37bd704_0_1_8C","u_0_1_O8",1],["__elem_fc9f538f_0_0_lD","u_0_2_tZ",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1g_dT","u_0_3_YS",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1h_JM","u_0_4_ew",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_k_+H","u_0_5_6\/",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1q_ey","u_0_6_UF",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_l_EE","u_0_7_7D",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1r_3p","u_0_8_kg",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_m_Q8","u_0_9_BJ",1],["__elem_072b8e64_0_1s_Tn","u_0_a_0n",1],["__elem_a588f507_0_n_\/R","